                "text": "**Analyzing Input Splits**\n\nMy focus has shifted to understanding the input structure for the two training sets. Train 1 splits a 10x20 input into two 10x10 halves (backgrounds 4 and 8), producing a 10x10 output. Train 2's 13x22 input is also being vertically split, with the left half being 13x...\n\n**Investigating Output Selection**\n\nI'm now analyzing why specific halves are chosen for output and the role of the background values. Train 1 uses the left, Train 2 the left, but Train 3 surprisingly picks the right. I'm trying to discern a pattern based on shapes and background values within these splits.\n\n**Identifying Key Matching**\n\nI'm refining my understanding of how \"key\" cells dictate copying. It seems a matching numerical value in one half's shape \"keys\" the paste operation to a corresponding cell in the other half. The absence of a matching key in an input half prevents its shape from being copied.\n\n**Confirming Key Logic**\n\nI've confirmed my hypothesis: a specific numerical value acts as a \"key.\" When shape cells in one half contain this key and a corresponding key exists in the other half, the shape is copied. The location of the keys dictates the paste destination.\n\n**Tracing Key Pasts**\n\nI'm tracing the exact \"key\" values and their locations. It appears that a \"1\" in the left half at row 8, column 6 is matched by a \"1\" in the right half at row 8, column 6. The \"2\" shapes from the right half were indeed pasted over the \"7\" shapes in the left.\n\n**Mapping Key Displacements**\n\nI've discovered that the positioning of '1' values within shapes in the top half directly corresponds to relative displacements of '1's in the bottom half shapes. This consistent offset seems to be the core mechanism for matching and copying elements between the two halves.\n\n**Mapping Shape Placements**\n\nI'm now mapping individual shape placements. For Shape 1, bottom half rows 15-17, columns 3-7 with '1's at (16,6) and (17,7) seem to map to top half rows 2-4, columns 4-8, with offsets of -13 rows and +1 column. Shape 2's '1' in the bottom half appears uncopied due to a lack of a corresponding key in the top half. Shape 3 in the bottom half is also being analyzed for placement.\n\n**Confirming Key Value Persistence**\n\nI've confirmed that the numerical values of the key cells themselves are retained in the output. Whether a '1', '7', or '8', the values that act as keys and the shapes they belong to are copied with their original numerical identities, maintaining their value in the destination.\n\n",
